# Creating an ECR Repository

After building the Docker image locally, the next step is creating an ECR repository.

The repository stores the Docker image in AWS.

A repository can be created in two ways:

1. Using AWS CLI
2. Using AWS Management Console

---

# Creating an ECR Repository Using AWS CLI

Use an AWS CLI command to create the repository.

Example:

```bash
aws ecr create-repository --repository-name node-monolith-app
```

If no region is specified in the command, AWS uses the default region configured in the CLI.

After running the command, AWS returns a success response confirming that the repository was created.

---

# Verifying the Repository in the Console

Open the ECR Console and refresh the repositories page.

The new repository should appear.

This confirms that the repository was created successfully using the CLI.

---

# Creating an ECR Repository from the Console

A repository can also be created directly from the AWS Management Console.

Steps:

1. Open the ECR Console.
2. Go to Repositories.
3. Click Create repository.
4. Enter a clear repository name.
5. Review image tag settings.
6. Review encryption settings.
7. Review image scanning settings.
8. Create the repository.

---

# Repository Name

The repository name should be clear and descriptive.

A good repository name helps identify the purpose of the image later.

---

# Image Tag Settings

Image tag settings control whether tags can be overwritten after an image is pushed.

This is called image tag mutability.

There are two main options:

- Mutable
- Immutable

---

# Mutable Tags

Mutable tags can be overwritten.

This means a new image can be pushed using the same tag and replace the previous tag reference.

Mutable tags are useful for pull-through cache workflows because ECR can update cached images.

---

# Immutable Tags

Immutable tags cannot be overwritten.

Once an image is pushed with a tag, that tag cannot be replaced.

This helps prevent accidental overwrites and provides stronger version safety.

Immutable tags are useful when you want each tag to always point to the same image.

---

# Mutable Tag Exclusion

Mutable tag exclusion allows specific tags to be excluded from immutability rules.

This provides more flexibility when some tags should remain mutable while others are protected.

For this setup, the default tag settings are used.

---

# Encryption Settings

Encryption settings define how repository data is encrypted at rest.

Amazon ECR supports:

1. AES-256
2. AWS KMS

---

# AES-256 Encryption

With AES-256, Amazon ECR uses server-side encryption with Amazon S3-managed encryption keys.

This uses the AES-256 algorithm.

This option comes at no additional cost.

---

# AWS KMS Encryption

With AWS KMS, ECR uses server-side encryption with AWS Key Management Service.

You can use:

- Default AWS-managed key
- Customer-managed key

A customer-managed key gives more control over encryption and access.

For this setup, the default encryption settings are used.

---

# Image Scanning Settings

Image scanning settings used to be configurable at the repository level.

This repository-level setting is now deprecated.

Image scanning is now managed at the registry level with repository filtering.

For this setup, the default image scanning settings are used.

---

# Authenticating to the ECR Registry

Before pushing an image to ECR, Docker must authenticate to the ECR registry.

Authentication connects the local Docker client securely to Amazon ECR.

This step is required before Docker can push or pull images from a private ECR repository.

---

# ECR Login Command

The AWS CLI is used to retrieve an ECR login password.

That password is passed to Docker login.

Example:

```bash
aws ecr get-login-password --region us-east-1 | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin <account-id>.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com
```

After the command succeeds, Docker is authenticated to the ECR registry.

---

# Pushing the Docker Image to ECR

After authentication, the next step is pushing the local Docker image to the ECR repository.

Before pushing, list the local Docker images:

```bash
docker images
```

The image built earlier should appear in the list.

---

# Tagging the Docker Image

Before pushing to ECR, the local image must be tagged with the ECR repository URI.

Image tagging gives the Docker image a label that identifies its target registry and repository.

This tells Docker where the image should be pushed.

Example:

```bash
docker tag node-monolith-app:latest <account-id>.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/node-monolith-app:latest
```

The tagged image is not a completely new image.

It is another reference or alias pointing to the same underlying image, but with a tag that points to the ECR repository.

---

# Verifying the Tagged Image

List local images again:

```bash
docker images
```

The output should show a new image entry using the ECR repository URI.

This confirms that the image was tagged correctly.

---

# Pushing the Image

Push the tagged image to ECR:

```bash
docker push <account-id>.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/node-monolith-app:latest
```

The first push may take longer because Docker uploads all image layers to ECR.

Future pushes are usually faster because Docker and ECR use layer caching.

Only changed layers need to be uploaded again.

---

# Verifying the Image in ECR Console

Open the ECR Console.

Go to Repositories.

Select the repository that was created earlier.

The pushed image should now appear inside the repository.

Open the image details to view information such as:

- Image tag
- Image URI
- Image digest
- Metadata

---

# Listing Images with AWS CLI

The image can also be verified from the terminal.

Example:

```bash
aws ecr list-images --repository-name node-monolith-app
```

The output should show an image with the tag:

```text
latest
```

This confirms that the image was pushed successfully.

---

# Pulling the Docker Image from ECR

The next step is pulling the image from ECR back into the local environment.

Before pulling, remove the tagged local image to avoid confusion.

---

# Removing the Local Tagged Image

List local Docker images:

```bash
docker images
```

Remove the tagged image:

```bash
docker rmi <account-id>.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/node-monolith-app:latest
```

List images again to confirm it was removed.

---

# Pulling the Image

Copy the image URI from the ECR Console.

Then pull the image:

```bash
docker pull <account-id>.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/node-monolith-app:latest
```

Docker downloads the image from ECR.

After the pull completes, the image is available locally again.

---

# Verifying the Pulled Image

List local Docker images:

```bash
docker images
```

The image pulled from ECR should appear in the local image list.

This confirms that the image can be retrieved successfully from Amazon ECR.

---

# Deleting the Docker Image from ECR

After verifying the push and pull workflow, the next step is deleting the image from ECR.

This removes the image from the repository.

Example:

```bash
aws ecr batch-delete-image \
  --repository-name node-monolith-app \
  --image-ids imageTag=latest
```

After running the command, AWS returns a confirmation response.

---

# Verifying Image Deletion

Open the ECR Console and refresh the repository images page.

The image should no longer appear.

You can also verify from the CLI:

```bash
aws ecr list-images --repository-name node-monolith-app
```

The output should show an empty list.

This confirms that the image was deleted successfully.

---

# Deleting the ECR Repository

The final step is deleting the repository itself.

Before deletion, list all repositories:

```bash
aws ecr describe-repositories
```

Then delete the repository:

```bash
aws ecr delete-repository --repository-name node-monolith-app
```

After running the command, AWS returns a success response with repository details.

---

# Verifying Repository Deletion

List repositories again:

```bash
aws ecr describe-repositories
```

The repository created through the CLI should no longer appear.

Open the ECR Console and refresh the repositories page.

The deleted repository should be gone.

This confirms that the repository was deleted successfully.

